A payoff allocation is <Pareto efficient> if no feasible allocation weakly increases every player's payoff and strictly increases at least one. Here feasibility requires total payment at most the available estate. The proposed payments have
$$
\frac83+\frac{14}3+\frac{23}3=15.
$$
Any such improvement would have total payment greater than $15$, which is impossible. Each payment is also between zero and its claim, and exceeds its singleton <coalition> value. Hence the allocation is an <imputation> and
$$
\boxed{\text{The allocation is Pareto optimal: (a) is true.}}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Shapley value> is the expected <marginal contribution> of a player when the players join in uniformly random order. Equivalently,
$$
\phi_i(v)=\sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}}\frac{|S|!(n-|S|-1)!}{n!}\bigl(v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S)\bigr).
$$
For three players, the weights for predecessor sets of sizes $0,1,2$ are $1/3,1/6,1/3$. Using the computed values,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\phi_1&=\tfrac13(0)+\tfrac16[(6-2)+(9-5)]+\tfrac13(15-11)=\tfrac83,\\
\phi_2&=\tfrac13(2)+\tfrac16[(6-0)+(11-5)]+\tfrac13(15-9)=\tfrac{14}3,\\
\phi_3&=\tfrac13(5)+\tfrac16[(9-0)+(11-2)]+\tfrac13(15-6)=\tfrac{23}3.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\phi(v)=x:\quad\text{(b) is true.}}
$$

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For an <imputation> $y$, the <excess of a coalition> is $e(S,y)=v(S)-\sum_{i\in S}y_i$. Arrange these excesses in decreasing order. The <nucleolus> is the <imputation> that lexicographically minimizes this ordered vector: minimize the largest complaint first, then the next largest among ties, and so on. The empty and grand-coalition excesses are fixed at zero for every efficient allocation, so they can be omitted without changing the lexicographic comparison.

For every efficient $y$ the three pair excesses have sum
$$
e(\{1,2\},y)+e(\{1,3\},y)+e(\{2,3\},y)=6+9+11-2(15)=-4.
$$
Hence at least one pair has excess at least $-4/3$, and the largest proper-coalition excess is at least $-4/3$. At the proposed allocation all three pair excesses equal $-4/3$, while all three singleton excesses equal $-8/3$. It therefore attains the best possible largest proper-coalition excess.

Moreover, any allocation attaining this bound must have all three pair excesses equal $-4/3$, since they are individually at most that value and sum to $-4$. This forces
$$
y_1+y_2=\frac{22}3,\qquad y_1+y_3=\frac{31}3,\qquad y_2+y_3=\frac{37}3,
$$
whose unique solution is $y=x$. It is already an <imputation>, so the first nonconstant lexicographic stage has a unique minimizer; there are no later choices to resolve. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{nuc}(v)=x:\quad\text{(c) is true.}}
$$

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <core of a cooperative game> consists of efficient allocations satisfying $\sum_{i\in S}x_i\geq v(S)$ for every <coalition>, so no <coalition> can obtain a greater total payoff by leaving. Efficiency has already been checked. For singletons,
$$
\frac83\geq0,\qquad\frac{14}3\geq2,\qquad\frac{23}3\geq5.
$$
For pairs,
$$
\frac{22}3\geq6,\qquad\frac{31}3\geq9,\qquad\frac{37}3\geq11.
$$
The empty constraint is automatic and the grand constraint is equality. Thus every core inequality holds and
$$
\boxed{x\in\operatorname{core}(v):\quad\text{(d) is true.}}
$$
All four assertions about this allocation are true, although their definitions impose different requirements.

